/* ============================================================
   Cigar Cafe — 価格帯別の英語本文
   英語版でだけ読み込まれ、CIGAR_DATA の該当項目を英語文に置き換える。
   日本語版はこのファイルを読まない。元データ: data/data.js + data/prices_deep.js
   ============================================================ */
(function () {
  if (typeof CIGAR_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var D = CIGAR_DATA;
  D.priceFactors = "The retail price of a cigar is not simply 'cost plus a profit margin'; it is built up from several structural cost factors layered on top of one another.\n【The cost gap in raw leaf】The single biggest variable is the cost of sourcing the wrapper leaf. Connecticut Shade, grown under cloth tents in the Connecticut River Valley of the United States to shield it from direct sun, yields little and demands a great deal of labour — the finest grades are said to fetch around $50–55 per pound — while shade-grown Honduran leaf runs to roughly $20–25 per pound and sun-grown broadleaf is said to be cheaper still, so the wrapper alone can open up a severalfold gap in raw-material cost. Because the wrapper governs most of a cigar's appearance and flavour, its grade feeds directly into the final price.\n【Labour intensity as a cost structure】At the heart of a premium cigar is the skill of the trained roller (torcedor) who performs 'totalmente a mano' (fully handmade) work. Becoming proficient takes years of training, yet industry reports cite Nicaraguan rollers earning less than $10 a day, and Cuban rollers are said to be paid even less. In other words, even when the finished cigar sells for thousands or tens of thousands of yen, only a tiny share of that goes to the manufacturing labour itself; what mainly pushes the price up is not labour cost but the brand, ageing and scarcity premiums described below. Machine-made dry cigars, by contrast, can automate most of the process and bring labour costs down to a nearly negligible level, which is what makes the low price band possible.\n【Ageing as a 'time cost'】Premium cigars require months of fermentation and ageing — for some marques on the scale of ten years. Throughout that period the maker bears the cost of climate-controlled warehousing and insurance, plus the opportunity cost of capital tied up in stock that could otherwise have been sold, and valuations are said to rise with each additional year of age (one cited rule of thumb is a premium of roughly 10–20% per year of ageing). Part of the price gap between the mid and upper classes is payment for this 'time spent resting the leaf' itself.\n【The brand and scarcity premium】The marques handled by Cuba's state-owned Habanos S.A. in particular take on the character of luxury goods whose demand is propped up by high prices (so-called Veblen goods), and output is deliberately restricted to preserve scarcity. Export volumes from Cuba have themselves been falling in recent years, and some observers note that import prices for certain marques have been rising by more than 20% a year on average.\n【Japan-specific taxes and regulation】Japan's tobacco tax charges cigarettes per stick, whereas cigars and pipe tobacco are, as a rule, taxed by weight, with one gram counted as the equivalent of one cigarette (under the 2020–2021 tax reforms, only lightweight cigars under one gram per stick — little cigars and the like — were switched to per-stick taxation, the same as cigarettes). As a result, hand-rolled premium cigars weighing several grams to over ten grams accumulate tax in proportion to their weight, while dry cigars under one gram carry only a light tax burden. The wide price valley between the low band and the mid-to-upper bands is thought to stem not only from differences in quality but from this tax structure. On top of that come import duty on cigars (a treaty rate of 16%) and 10% consumption tax, and under the Tobacco Business Law (たばこ事業法) every tobacco product may only be sold at a retail price approved by the Minister of Finance, with no discounting allowed. Ministry of Finance review papers note that some importers have given up on importing altogether because the approved prices leave no margin, so the price level of fine cigars in Japan — and the limited range on offer — reflects not just tariffs and exchange rates but this approval system as well.";
  D.dryVsPremium = "The price gap between dry cigars (including little cigars) and premium cigars reflects not a mere difference in 'quality grade' but two fundamentally different economic models of production.\nA dry cigar is an industrial product: dried tobacco leaf is cut and formed by machine and mass-produced in a short time. Most weigh under one gram per stick, so under Japanese tax law they are taxed per stick in the same way as cigarettes, keeping the tax burden light; they need no humidity control, keep at room temperature and are sold through convenience stores. Combined with the low share of labour cost in manufacturing, this makes possible a price band of roughly ¥25–750 per stick — close to that of cigarettes.\nA premium cigar, by contrast, is the product of a labour-intensive artisan industry: selected natural leaves are rolled by hand by skilled rollers into a three-layer structure of filler, binder and wrapper. The cost of the wrapper leaf alone varies severalfold with origin and cultivation method, and most cigars spend months to years — sometimes on the scale of a decade — in fermentation and ageing, so makers carry long-term inventory costs (storage, insurance, opportunity cost). In Japan, on top of that, weight-based tobacco tax (one gram = one cigarette equivalent), 16% import duty, consumption tax and the retail price approval system all pile up, so prices typically end up far higher than in the country of origin. Sticks weighing over ten grams are not unusual — dry cigars and premium cigars differ by an order of magnitude in the very weight on which they are taxed.\nThe result is a price gap that can reach tens of times, sometimes on the order of a hundred times. But this is not simply a matter of 'expensive versus cheap': it is the difference between machine-based mass production and small-scale handcraft — in coffee terms, as different as instant coffee and a speciality hand-poured brew. For a beginner, the sensible entry point on grounds of both cost and risk is to get used to the rhythm of smoking with dry cigars first, then move on to entry-level premium cigars if the taste appeals.";
  var TIERS = [
  { tier: "Dry cigars / little cigars", range: "roughly ¥25–750",
    feat: "Industrial products in which tobacco leaf is cut and rolled by machine, most weighing under one gram per stick. Because most of the process can be automated, the share of labour cost is extremely low, and under Japanese tax law they are taxed per stick in the same way as cigarettes, keeping the tax burden light — all of which pushes prices down to cigarette levels. They need no humidity control, keep at room temperature and are sold even in convenience stores. Smoking time is a short 5–15 minutes, and being able to light up and smoke straight away is their greatest appeal. The band includes filtered 'little cigars' close to cigarettes and small unfiltered 'dry cigars (cigarillos)'.",
    whom: "Anyone who wants to try a cigar for the first time, does not want to buy accessories, or fancies a quick, casual smoke.",
    advice: "Start your first cigar here. Many marques need no tools, so the risk of failure is low. Begin by getting used to 'puffing' — holding the smoke in the mouth to taste it, without inhaling. Drawing hard and fast makes it easy to cough, so go slowly, a little at a time.",
    sections: [
      { h: "How mechanisation keeps costs down",
        body: "【Machine-made production】Unlike premium cigars rolled one by one by skilled artisans, dry cigars and little cigars are essentially 'machine-made', cut and rolled at high speed by dedicated machinery. Rather than long filler, which runs whole leaves lengthways through the cigar, they typically use 'short filler' — chopped scraps of leaf — and because the machines standardise the fill, the draw and burn tend to be stable, though the flavour is said to lack the complexity of a hand-rolled cigar.\n【Homogenised tobacco leaf (HTL)】In cheaper products, the wrapper or binder may be 'homogenised tobacco leaf (HTL)': tobacco dust, scraps and stems collected and extruded into sheets with cellulose-based binders. According to the trade magazine Cigar Aficionado, HTL spread in the 1950s as a cost-cutting technique; it lets makers reintegrate offcuts that would otherwise be discarded, cutting raw-material costs substantially (Cigar Aficionado, House of Horvath). This mechanisation and use of offcuts is the foundation on which the low price band rests." },
      { h: "Why Japanese tax law keeps this band cheap, and the little-cigar tax fix",
        body: "【Taxation by weight as the rule】Japan's tobacco tax charges cigarettes per stick, whereas cigars are, as a rule, taxed by weight, with one gram converted to one cigarette equivalent. A dry cigar under one gram per stick attracts little tax under this conversion, so its tax burden is structurally lower than that of a thick, heavy hand-rolled premium.\n【The little-cigar tax fix】'Little cigars' that looked just like cigarettes were once taxed on the same weight-conversion basis, in some cases attracting around ¥5 of tax per stick against roughly ¥13 for a cigarette, and they spread as a cheap substitute (Nihon Keizai Shimbun, 2019). To correct this, from 1 October 2020 lightweight cigars were switched to a one-stick-equals-one-cigarette basis, with a transitional measure until 30 September 2021 counting cigars under 0.7 grams as 0.7 sticks, raising the tax in stages (Ministry of Finance, National Tax Agency). As a result little cigars rose to roughly ¥500 or so per pack, and the price gap with cigarettes is said to have largely disappeared (SUPARI). Traditional unfiltered dry cigars, meanwhile, remain in the lightweight bracket and comparatively cheap." },
      { h: "The fundamental difference between dry cigars and premiums",
        body: "【Two economic models of production】The difference between dry cigars and premium cigars is not merely a grade difference in quality but reflects two fundamentally different economic structures: machine-based mass production versus small-scale handcraft. A premium is hand-rolled by a skilled roller from selected natural leaves into three layers — filler, binder and wrapper — and spends months to years in fermentation and ageing; a dry cigar is formed from dried leaf by machine and finished in a short time.\n【Storage and flavour】Where a premium needs humidity management at around 70% relative humidity, a dry cigar is, as the name suggests, made to remain stable in a dry state and can be kept at room temperature with no special care. That said, since 'a proper dry cigar is also 100% tobacco leaf, in that respect no different from a premium', some hold that humidity control improves the taste (e-amanoya). The layering and length of the flavour usually fall short of a premium, but that very convenience and stability is this band's reason for being — better understood as a difference in purpose than in merit." },
      { h: "Representative marques and what they actually taste like",
        body: "【Tabacalera Half Corona】A dry cigar made with leaf from the Philippines (Manila), measuring about 105 mm × 14 mm — around half a corona. Known as an heir to the Manila cigars Churchill favoured, it is said to offer a rounded, richly deep taste. A vacuum treatment at the final stage of manufacture to eliminate tobacco beetles is also cited as a feature.\n【Clubmaster / Villiger】Clubmaster is made in Germany from Brazilian and Sumatran leaf, in a standard 75 mm length and a 67 mm 'Mini'. Villiger is a Swiss house founded in 1888 that ranges from hand-rolled premiums to high-quality machine-made cigars. Both can be smoked in a short sitting and pair easily with a coffee break.\n【Little cigars】Forte, Camel, Double Happiness and the like are filtered and close to cigarettes in form. Sold mainly in packs of twenty, they work out at a few dozen yen per stick, forming the cheapest layer of this band. The taste is on the light side, and flavoured products are common." },
      { h: "Where this band fits as a first step, and points to watch",
        body: "【A rational entry point】Needing no tools, no fussy storage, and priced close to cigarettes, dry cigars and little cigars are a rational gateway to cigars on grounds of both cost and risk. Get used to the rhythm of smoking and the sense of 'tasting the smoke' here, and if you take to it, move on to an entry-level hand-rolled premium — a natural, unforced progression.\n【How to smoke】The basic technique is 'puffing' (mouth smoking): savour the smoke in the mouth without taking it into the lungs. Drawing hard and fast raises the temperature at the burning end, brings out harshness, and readily causes throat sting, coughing and dizziness, so the knack is to take it slowly, a little at a time (SMOPIA). Little cigars in particular, being filtered and cigarette-like, tempt smokers to inhale, but they are said to kick harder than cigarettes and to upset the system more easily, so keeping strictly to mouth smoking is considered the safe course. None of this removes the health risks; the premise remains that these are indulgences to be enjoyed in moderation." }
    ] },
  { tier: "Entry class", range: "roughly ¥800–1,850",
    feat: "The most accessible band of true premiums rolled entirely by hand — 'totalmente a mano' (roughly ¥800–1,850 as a guide, varying with timing and exchange rates). The mainstays are marques using Nicaraguan and Honduran leaf: both countries have roller wage levels relatively lower than Cuba's, and most products ship on comparatively short ageing cycles of a few months to a few years rather than relying on decade-scale ageing, which keeps prices down despite being handmade. Light to medium and easy to smoke, this band offers a first 'way in' to the layered flavour, finish and sweetness that a mere machine-made dry cigar does not have.",
    whom: "Beginners stepping up from machine-made cigars to try a genuine, fully hand-rolled premium for the first time. Also suits those who want to learn flavour complexity, or to enjoy handmade cigars cost-effectively as a weekday daily smoke.",
    advice: "You are unlikely to go wrong with a gentle mild-to-medium-bodied Nicaraguan or Honduran. For size, start with a slim corona or a short robusto that smokes in a manageable 45–60 minutes. Try a few marques one stick at a time, get a feel for your preferred origin and strength, and only then move on to buying by the box.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why handmade yet affordable — wage levels and ageing cycles",
        body: "Fully hand-rolled cigars rest on the craft of trained rollers (torcedores) in a labour-intensive industry, yet the share of that labour in the final price is surprisingly small. The Louisiana Farm Bureau in the United States reported (2014) that 'the artisan rolling a $25 cigar earns a daily wage of $9', and monthly pay for leaf-tobacco workers in Nicaragua in recent years is put at roughly $130–310. In other words, even on a cigar selling for several thousand yen only a sliver goes to manufacturing labour, and the relatively low wage levels of Nicaragua and Honduras are the first reason a hand-rolled cigar can sell cheaply.\n【Short ageing cycles】The other key is the ageing period. Where leaf for the upper classes rests a further three to five years after fermentation — sometimes on a ten-year scale — most of the entry band ships on a comparatively short cycle: a few months of fermentation plus a few months to a few years after rolling. With warehousing, insurance and the opportunity cost of tied-up capital all held down, the 'time cost' added to the price stays small. It is also consistent with this cycle that leaf in the budget band is not generally designed to be transformed by long ageing." },
      { h: "Beginner-friendly origins and blend tendencies",
        body: "The heart of this band is marques using Nicaraguan and Honduran leaf. Leaf from both countries naturally carries low-slung, sturdy flavours — cedar, leather, roasted notes, nuts, bitter chocolate, pepper — but entry lines mostly rein in the strength, rounding off the bite just after lighting with mild-to-medium leaning blends.\n【Putting gentle wrappers to work】Many marques use calmer Ecuadorian or Connecticut-seed leaf for the wrapper, which shapes the feel on the palate. Connecticut Shade-style wrappers offer cream, nuts and light cedar, and English-language beginner guides call them the smoothest introduction, one that 'teaches your palate without attacking it'.\n【Character differences by origin】As a general schema, Dominican leaf is the gentlest — sweet and creamy; Nicaraguan is powerful and complex; Honduran sits in between, combining earthiness with nutty aromas. Choosing the mellower strains within this band lets you enjoy the complexity without being overwhelmed by strength." },
      { h: "A first stick without failure — choosing size and strength",
        body: "The first thing a beginner should mind is 'strength' (body): mild to medium, with gentle nicotine impact and bite, is considered the safe zone. Full-bodied marques smoked on an empty stomach readily bring on dizziness and queasiness (nicotine sickness) and are unsuited to a first cigar.\n【Keep the size moderate】Length and thickness (ring gauge) matter too: a robusto (around 5 inches × 50) or corona that finishes in about 45–60 minutes, or a slim panetela, is easy to handle. Extremely thick marques, or two-hour churchills and double coronas, are a heavy lift for a beginner in terms of managing the burn and holding concentration.\n【Construction decides the experience】English-language primers urge weighting 'good construction that burns evenly' even above flavour preferences. A crudely made cigar tunnels, burns lopsidedly and keeps going out, leaving the smoker doubting their own technique. Choosing a stick from a trusted brand that burns evenly even when lit a little carelessly is what leads to that first successful experience." },
      { h: "Representative marques of this band and what they are",
        body: "【Flor de Copán】Rolled entirely by hand in a colonial-era building that serves as the factory in Santa Rosa de Copán, western Honduras; now owned by Altadis USA. A medium body laying light sweetness over cocoa, coffee, nuts and cedar, praised even by European retailers as 'an ideal starting point for newcomers' — the textbook example of this band.\n【Plasencia Original】A certified organic cigar from the Plasencias, one of the world's largest leaf-growing families. US retail reference prices are around $7.90 for the robusto and $8.90 for the toro (varying by outlet and timing), which in yen terms lands broadly within this band. Its strength is consistent quality backed by vertical integration from farming through manufacture.\n【My Father】The flagship marques of the García family of Estelí, Nicaragua. The flagship Original and Le Bijou 1922 lean medium-full to full and usually sit above this band, but there are also gentler, comparatively affordable lines such as the Connecticut, giving newcomers a window onto the house's capabilities." },
      { h: "What the entry band delivers — and its limits",
        body: "The greatest value of this band is a low-risk taste of the 'fully hand-rolled experience' no machine-made dry cigar can give: the layers of flavour created by the three-part construction, the taste slowly shifting as it develops, and the finish that lingers after the final draw — the basic grammar of the premium cigar can be learnt here. The value for money that lets you light up daily without a second thought is another attraction, making it an ideal 'practice ground' for narrowing down your preferred origin, strength and size.\n【Limits, and the step beyond】What this price cannot fully deliver is the mellow roundness born of long ageing and the complexity and smoothness a top-grade wrapper brings. Budget-band leaf generally does not transform much however long it rests, so do not expect too much from long ageing at home either. Train your palate and smoking rhythm in the entry band first, and once the differences become clear, move up to the middle class and beyond — the rational path on both cost and satisfaction." }
    ] },
  { tier: "Middle class", range: "roughly ¥2,000–3,500",
    feat: "The core band where the standard lines of world-famous brands meet Cuba's flagship marques. What pushes prices up here is less the raw-material cost itself than structural costs: (1) the quality-control apparatus that keeps mass production at consistent quality, (2) the inventory and storage costs of medium-term ageing over several years, and (3) Japan's weight-proportional taxation (cigars are taxed at roughly one gram = one cigarette equivalent, so thicker, heavier sizes carry proportionally more tax). Repeated price rises driven by tight Cuban supply and demand have lately pushed up the floor of this band. Medium-bodied, offering a balance of sweetness with origin-driven complexity — earth, leather, nuts, cacao, honey.",
    whom: "Intermediate smokers who have begun to tell flavours apart, those who want to cover the brand staples, and anyone looking for a treat or a stick to offer guests.",
    advice: "Montecristo No. 4, one of the world's best-selling cigars, and the spicy, deep Partagás Serie D No. 4 are consistently rated and serve as flavour benchmarks for intermediates. Smoking a Cuban staple against a Nicaraguan or Dominican staple in the same band is the easiest way to grasp the differences of origin.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What drives prices up in this band",
        body: "The step up in price from the entry class comes less from dearer leaf itself than from supply stability and time costs.\n【The apparatus behind consistent quality】The standard lines of famous brands must keep supplying hundreds of thousands to millions of sticks a year worldwide without interruption, which requires real spending on a quality-control apparatus: reproducible leaf selection and blending, management of skilled rollers, and sampling inspections. The machinery that guarantees 'the same taste whenever you buy' is itself built into the price.\n【Medium-term ageing as a time cost】Much of this band rests for several further years after fermentation before shipping, incurring climate-controlled warehousing and insurance over that period plus the opportunity cost of idle capital.\n【Weight-proportional taxation】In Japan cigars are, as a rule, taxed by weight at one gram = one cigarette equivalent, so from a robusto upwards the tax bill climbs in proportion to size. On top of that, tight Cuban supply has brought repeated price rises in recent years — Montecristo No. 4's Japanese list price is said to have reached about ¥3,190 in the 2024 revision — lifting the very floor of the band (subject to change over time)." },
      { h: "Cuban staples versus New World staples",
        body: "Even at the same ¥2,000–3,500, the design philosophy of the flavour splits sharply by origin.\n【Cuba (Habanos)】Made with leaf from Vuelta Abajo, built on earth, cedar and nuts, with the distinctive note aficionados call 'twang' (a sharp earthiness). Medium overall and balanced, with a natural sweetness riding the finish.\n【Nicaragua】Volcanic soil rich in nitrogen and potassium produces a powerful, spicy body suggesting pepper, leather, espresso and dark chocolate. In this band the staples from Oliva and the AJ Fernández stable qualify, delivering punchy flavour at a modest price.\n【Dominican Republic】Leaf raised in the limestone Cibao Valley tends smooth and mild, with a faint sweetness over nuts, cream and cedar; the ash also tends to hold white and firm.\nIn short: Cuba for complexity and balance, Nicaragua for strength and depth, the Dominican for smoothness and delicacy — this is the price band where the 'textbook differences' of the three origins can be experienced most affordably." },
      { h: "The flagship marques in practice",
        body: "Knowing the benchmark staples of this band gives you a yardstick for taste.\n【Montecristo No. 4】A petit corona of RG 42 × about 129 mm. Sometimes called the best-selling Havana in the world — the staple of staples — with a smooth, rich smoke suggesting roast coffee, cacao, caramel and vanilla. It is treated as the benchmark against which every petit corona is compared.\n【Partagás Serie D No. 4】A robusto of RG 50 × about 129 mm. One of Cuba's most popular marques, established around 2011, it layers generous spice and Cuban sweetness with cacao, cedar and nuts from the first light, adding floral elements later as it builds from medium to medium-full. The marriage of power and complexity endears it to intermediates.\n【Romeo y Julieta / H. Upmann】Romeo is a classicist with the fruit of cherry and plum and a caramel sweetness. Upmann, using Vuelta Abajo leaf, is a light-to-medium, elegant smoke, well suited to a gentle stick in the morning or before a meal." },
      { h: "How intermediates learn to tell flavours apart",
        body: "Choose in this band with the three variables — origin, strength, size — in mind and the resolution of your palate jumps at once.\n【Choose by origin】First smoke one Cuban staple and one Nicaraguan or Dominican staple under the same conditions; the presence or absence of twang and the way the spice emerges become plainly clear. Grasping the outline of each origin is the fastest way to improve.\n【Step up through strengths】Enter through the light-to-medium Upmann or the Dominicans, move to the medium Montecristo No. 4, then to the medium-full Partagás Serie D No. 4 — raising the bar little by little lets you follow the differences in body without confusion.\n【Size and time】A petit corona (about 30–40 minutes) finishes comfortably even on a weekday, while a robusto (about 45–60 minutes) makes it easier to savour the arc of the flavour. Comparing a slim and a thick vitola from the same brand also lets you feel how ring gauge affects burn temperature and intensity of taste. Avoid an empty stomach, and check the contours of the flavour with water or coffee alongside." },
      { h: "How to think about value for money",
        body: "In this band, 'value' is best measured not by cheapness per stick but by the repeatability of satisfaction for the price.\n【The reassurance of the staples】The standard lines of famous brands vary little in quality, so duds are rare. If you want dependable satisfaction for a first stick or for guests, that repeatability is itself the value.\n【Cuba for flavour, New World for volume】Cuban staples tend to run dear even at the same price — tax, duty and approved list prices pile up — but can be accepted as the cost of a flavour found nowhere else. Nicaraguan and Dominican staples, by contrast, offer equal or better finish more affordably, and suit those who want to smoke more sticks day to day.\n【A yardstick for the bands above】Sampling several origins and strengths in this band equips you to judge, when buying premium and higher lines, 'whether that price difference means anything to you'. Broadening your range across this band first is better value than blindly climbing to dearer marques. Prices shift with revisions, so it is prudent to check the official licensed retailer's list price at the time of purchase." }
    ] },
  { tier: "Premium class", range: "roughly ¥3,500–7,000",
    feat: "The luxury lines where brand power, raw materials, ageing and rolling precision all come together. The raw cost of fine wrappers such as good Connecticut Shade, the inventory cost of long ageing — three to five years for filler, up to eight to ten for wrapper — and then Japan's import duty, tobacco tax and approved list prices stack up, so this band tends to land well above overseas prices. Cuba's 'Cohiba' and the upper lines of the Dominican grandee 'Davidoff' take the lead, with thickly layered flavours and a long finish.",
    whom: "Devotees who can relish the differences in flavour, a stick for a special day, or a gift for a superior.",
    advice: "A stick to be 'savoured slowly over one to two hours'. It shows its true worth only when stored at around 18°C and 70% humidity. For gifts and large purchases, buy from official licensed retailers — which never discount — to avoid fakes.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The cost structure of this band — fine wrappers and long ageing",
        body: "The first factor pushing up the price is the quality of the wrapper (the outer rolling leaf), the most expensive part of a cigar. The top grades of 'Connecticut Shade', grown under sun-blocking cheesecloth, are said to reach around $45–55 per pound wholesale and to be one of the most labour-intensive, riskiest and costliest crops there is (Cigar Aficionado). Allowing for the yield once damaged leaves are discarded, the wrapper cost alone can reportedly reach $0.50 per cigar, and over $1 for demanding luxury marques.\n【Ageing and inventory cost】The second factor is ageing. Filler and binder may rest three to five years and wrappers eight to ten in some cases, and the journey from harvest to shop shelf is said to average about three years. The climate-controlled warehousing over that period, and the opportunity cost of capital locked into stock, are added to the price. More than raw-material cost itself, it is this investment in time and quality control that underpins this band's prices." },
      { h: "Why Japan runs dearer — tax, duty and approved list prices",
        body: "The same marque costs more on a Japanese shelf than abroad because several public levies pile on top of one another. Import duty on cigars is 16% at the treaty rate (20% at the general rate; leaf from Mexico, Indonesia and the Philippines is exempt under EPAs), with tobacco tax and 10% consumption tax on top (Customs materials). Because tobacco tax on cigars is levied by weight — one gram of cigar counted as one cigarette — thicker, heavier sizes carry proportionally more tax.\n【An approved list price with no discounting】Beyond that, cigar retail prices in Japan operate under a 'list price' system approved by the Minister of Finance: the same price at every shop, with no discounting. Importers apply for list prices that factor in duty, tax, logistics and exchange rates, so in a weak-yen phase list prices tend to rise with each revision. In 2024 the Ministry of Finance deliberated on the future of the retail list-price system for premium cigars." },
      { h: "Where Cohiba stands — Cuba's flagship brand",
        body: "Cohiba, Cuba's pinnacle brand, was born in 1966 at the El Laguito factory in Havana, initially as a non-commercial cigar for Chairman Castro and other dignitaries; commercial sale began in 1982. Known for leaf from hand-picked fields and a distinctive extra fermentation in its process, it carries international renown and a price premium as the 'face' of Cuban cigars.\n【The Siglo line as the entry to this band】In Japan the classic 'Siglo' series is the gateway to this band: the Siglo I (petit corona), for instance, is said to sit in the high ¥6,000s (as of 2025, varying over time). The thicker Siglo II and above exceed ¥8,000 after revisions, reaching into the high-end band. Because brand value and scarcity account for a substantial share of the final price, this brand tends to cost more than non-Cuban cigars of the same size." },
      { h: "Where Davidoff's upper lines stand — the Dominican grandee",
        body: "Davidoff is a brand built on a celebrated Geneva tobacco shop. It once made Cuban cigars at the same El Laguito factory as Cohiba, but dissatisfaction with quality led to a break with Cuba in 1989–1991 and a wholesale move to the Dominican Republic in partnership with the master Hendrik Kelner.\n【Grand Cru and Signature】In the current upper lines, the balanced Dominican 'Grand Cru' No. 2 runs around ¥4,400 a stick and the lighter 'Signature' No. 2 around ¥4,500 (both 2025 Japanese list prices, varying over time). Milder than Cuban Cohiba, uniform and polished in finish, Davidoff is prized as a dependable staple that is easy to choose even as a gift, because duds are so rare." },
      { h: "Choosing for gifts and special days — and how not to slip up",
        body: "For a gift, the practical approach is to choose by the smoking time and body the recipient can actually manage. Much of this band is meant to be savoured over one to two hours, so a shorter robusto or corona is the safe choice for someone pressed for time. For a superior, Cohiba Siglo and Davidoff Grand Cru are the classic picks for renown and reliability.\n【Storage, and telling real from fake】Store at around 18°C and around 70% humidity; a dried-out cigar cracks its wrapper and loses its aroma, so both giver and recipient can rest easier with a humidor or an enclosed Boveda pack. Be warned that fakes circulate readily at this price and above — for Cubans in particular, forged bands and box codes have been reported. In Japan, where discount selling does not exist, an extreme bargain is if anything a warning sign; the iron rule is to buy from official licensed retailers or brand boutiques." }
    ] },
  { tier: "High end / top class", range: "roughly ¥7,000+",
    feat: "The topmost lines, built on rare leaf, long ageing and limited production (broadly ¥7,000 or more per stick, ranging up to the tens of thousands). On top of raw-material and ageing costs, supply is deliberately restricted to preserve scarcity, so the pricing strategy of a luxury good whose demand is propped up by high prices (a Veblen good) shows through strongly. The emblematic Cohiba Behike BHK56 is said to have reached a Japanese list price of ¥57,200 per stick in the May 2025 revision (Japan Cigar Association; changes with price revisions). Most of the price pays for brand value and scarcity, and with it comes complexity, weight and an overwhelming finish.",
    whom: "Seasoned devotees with well-trained palates, collectors, and anyone seeking a 'once-in-a-lifetime' stick for an anniversary, a closed deal or a celebration. Also the summit of gift-giving.",
    advice: "Its true worth is best savoured after training your palate in the middle and premium bands. This is also the band where fakes circulate most: buying from official retailers licensed by the Ministry of Finance (in Japan, in line with Habanos Japan list prices) is the iron rule. Discount offers, glass-top boxes and improbable 'bargains' should be presumed fake.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What the money buys — rare leaf, time and brand, in three layers",
        body: "This band's prices cannot be explained by raw-material cost alone. The first thing paid for is rare leaf. The 'medio tiempo' used in Cohiba Behike consists of small, oil-rich leaves that only occasionally sprout at the very top of the tobacco plant, above the ligero; they catch the most sun and yield the densest, most complex sweetness. Yet only about one plant in ten is said to produce usable leaves, and each such plant yields a mere two (Cigar Aficionado). The second is time: the upper lines take years — up to a decade — of fermentation and ageing, and the storage, insurance and opportunity cost of capital over that period are built into the valuation. The third is brand and exclusivity: Cuba's state-owned Habanos pursues a Veblen-good strategy, restricting output to preserve scarcity.\n【Most of the price pays for scarcity】The hand-rollers' wages are said to be a tiny fraction of the final price; most of what you pay goes to the intangible value of leaf, time and brand." },
      { h: "The summit in practice — Cohiba Behike (BHK) 52/54/56",
        body: "Behike, launched for Cohiba's 40th anniversary (2010), is the pinnacle line and is said to be the only Habanos to blend in medio tiempo. It comes in three sizes — BHK52 (robusto class), 54 and 56 (toro class) — growing stronger and weightier as they thicken. Overseas retail guide prices put the BHK52 at about $263, the 54 at about $328 and the 56 at about $365 (Cigar Aficionado; changes with price revisions). In recent years Habanos has repeatedly raised prices on an average scale of around 50%, citing the imbalance between supply and demand.\n【The Japanese list price】In Japan the BHK56's list price is said to have reached ¥57,200 per stick in the May 2025 revision (Japan Cigar Association; subject to change). At ring gauge 56 and 166 mm long, it smokes for roughly two hours. Cocoa, espresso and leather come in layers, with a dark sweetness from the medio tiempo and an overwhelming finish; it is counted among the hardest cigars in the world to obtain." },
      { h: "A diplomatic legacy and a grandee's limited editions — Trinidad and Davidoff",
        body: "Trinidad was born in 1969 at the storied El Laguito factory and long remained a closely guarded marque that Fidel Castro handed out as diplomatic gifts. Public sale began in 1998, first at a ceremony at the Habana Libre Hotel, initially in a single size, the 'Fundadores' (Wikipedia / Cigar Aficionado). Drawing a refined sweetness over a core of chocolate and coffee, it stands in Cohiba's shadow yet is highly esteemed as a connoisseur's top-tier choice.\n【The non-Cuban summit — Davidoff】The Swiss grandee Davidoff covers this band with limited and commemorative collections. The revived 'Aniversario No. 1' limited edition is said to carry a US MSRP of $64 per stick, limited to 6,500 boxes worldwide, and the Winston Churchill Limited Edition 2025 about $490 per stick (halfwheel and others; subject to change). In using only the very top grades of leaf and strictly limiting production, it shares its approach with the Cuban elite." },
      { h: "Counterfeit risk and the iron rule of buying official",
        body: "Expensive and famous, Cohiba — the Behike above all — is said to be the most counterfeited cigar in the world; even on the streets of Havana, genuine and fake are said to circulate in roughly equal numbers (Cigar Aficionado). Street, beach and tourist-spot vendors, and 'bargains' far below the official list price, can be presumed fake.\n【What to check】No genuine Habanos comes with a glass lid or clear box — a transparent-lidded box is always a fake. On a genuine band, the Taíno-head hologram shows a double 'head within a head' image, with precise gold colouring and printing. The box carries the Cuban warranty seal and a matching barcode for verification, checkable at habanos.com. The roll should be even with no gaps; a spongy softness or a misshapen cap is a danger sign.\n【The iron rule】In Japan, buying at Japanese list prices from official retailers licensed by the Ministry of Finance is the only certain route. Put provenance before price." },
      { h: "Value as a collectible, and Japan's shifting availability",
        body: "The upper lines are at once an indulgence and something of an asset: some marques rise in value with proper ageing, and so are laid down by the box for collecting or investment. Bringing out their true worth presupposes stable storage at around 18°C and around 70% humidity; in poor conditions even the finest cigar loses its value.\n【Japan's particular wall of availability】In Japan the Tobacco Business Law requires every marque to be sold only at a retail list price approved by the Minister of Finance, with no discounting whatsoever. Ministry of Finance review papers note that some businesses give up on importing altogether because the approved prices leave no margin, and on top of that come 16% import duty, weight-proportional tobacco tax, consumption tax and exchange rates. The result is that prices tend to land above overseas list prices, rare marques that cannot pay their way are sometimes dropped, and availability shifts from year to year." }
    ] },
  /* TIERS_END */
  ];
  if (D.priceTiers.length === TIERS.length)
    D.priceTiers.forEach(function (t, i) {
      var e = TIERS[i];
      t.tier = e.tier; t.range = e.range; t.feat = e.feat; t.whom = e.whom; t.advice = e.advice;
      if (t.sections && e.sections && t.sections.length === e.sections.length)
        t.sections.forEach(function (s, j) { s.h = e.sections[j].h; s.body = e.sections[j].body; });
    });
  var TOPICS = [
  { h: "Japanese taxes, regulation and cigar prices",
    body: "【The Tobacco Business Law and its licensing system】Cigar pricing in Japan is set within the framework of the Tobacco Business Law (たばこ事業法), enacted in 1984 and in force from 1985. The system has three tiers: (1) to import and wholesale, a business needs registration as a 'specified seller' (specified seller of manufactured tobacco) from the Minister of Finance (Article 11 of the Law); (2) to sell to consumers, a 'retail licence' is needed for each place of business (Article 22); and (3) to sell any product not previously on sale, approval of its 'retail list price' is needed item by item (Article 33). Unregistered import sales are illegal, and the only outlets that can legitimately stock cigars — high-street cigar shops, cigar bars, convenience stores — are businesses holding these credentials.\n【Cigars taxed by weight (1 g = 1 cigarette)】Tobacco tax comprises three streams — national tobacco tax, local tobacco tax (prefectural plus municipal) and the special tobacco tax — with 10% consumption tax layered on at purchase. The tax base for cigars is, as a rule, computed by converting one gram of weight to one cigarette, and total tobacco tax per cigarette after the October 2021 increase comes to roughly ¥15.2 (¥15,244 per 1,000 sticks) (Ministry of Finance materials, as of 2021). The heavier the cigar (that is, the more leaf it uses), the more tax it accrues, in direct proportion.\n【How the little-cigar loophole was closed】Under the one-gram conversion, 'little cigars' — slim, cigarette-like and light per stick — carried a comparatively low tax burden and for a time circulated cheaply in volume. The government moved to correct this in the FY2020 and FY2021 reforms, phasing in minimum conversions — from October 2020 a cigar under 0.7 grams counted as 0.7 cigarettes, and from October 2021 a cigar under one gram counted as one cigarette — with transitional measures to soften the change, largely closing the tax gap between lightweight cigars and cigarettes (government and ruling-party policy; 2019 press reports and others).\n【Import duty and consumption tax】Imported cigars attract customs duty at the border on top of tobacco tax. For cigars, cigarillos and the like (HS 2402.10) the treaty rate is 16% and the general rate around 20% (Customs tariff schedule, revised from time to time), with 10% domestic consumption tax added after clearance. This stacking of tobacco tax, duty and consumption tax, layer upon layer, is a major reason cigars in Japan tend to cost more than abroad.\n【Approved list prices, no discounting, and discontinued imports】Retailers may not sell other than at the approved retail list price (Article 33), and selling off-price — including de facto price cuts via free gifts — is in principle not permitted (Article 36; Ministry of Finance FAQ). Hence the same marque costs the same anywhere in the country. The flip side is that for low-demand marques the approval procedure and the costs of stock and storage may not pay their way, and imports or listings are sometimes discontinued.\n【Tax on personal imports】Importing for one's own use is itself possible, but 'tobacco' imported by international post and the like is subject to tobacco tax and the special tobacco tax regardless of quantity or price, with no exemption even when the customs value is ¥10,000 or less (Customs). Importing for resale requires the specified-seller registration described above. Transit also means no humidity control en route, with attendant risks of drying, mould and damage. Note: tax rates, conversion rules, list prices and duties are all subject to revision — check the latest information from the Ministry of Finance, the National Tax Agency, Customs and the like before buying or importing." },
  { h: "Exchange rates, distribution and 'why Japan runs dearer'",
    body: "【Who carries official distribution, and agents' margins】Selling cigars legitimately in Japan requires the importing business to be registered with the Minister of Finance, the retailer to hold a licence for each place of business, and each product to have an approved 'retail list price' (Tobacco Business Law). For Cuban Habanos, official supply runs through the distributor network for the Asian region, with official domestic operators handling wholesale and retail and running the 'La Casa del Habano' shops. Davidoff and the Dominican brands are likewise imported through their respective official domestic agents. At each stage — producing country, exporter, importing trading house, retailer — freight, insurance, customs clearance and each layer's margin pile up, and this forms the base of the gap with local prices.\n【The effect of exchange rates】Cigars are, as a rule, bought in in dollars or euros, so a weak yen feeds straight into procurement costs. The dollar–yen rate briefly touched the ¥161 range in July 2024, averaged about ¥151 across 2024, and stood in the ¥156–159 range at the end of 2025 — historically weak levels for the yen (various exchange-rate statistics, as of 2024–2025). Exchange rates move daily, and in a weak-yen phase domestic list prices tend to ratchet up with each revision.\n【The running costs of climate control and inventory】A premium cigar is close to a perishable good, demanding management at around 18°C and around 70% humidity, which means electricity and equipment costs for running walk-in humidor air-conditioning and humidification the year round. The interest and storage burden of holding slow-moving, high-priced stock as long-term inventory is also passed through to the final price.\n【Market size and economies of scale】Japan's population of devotees is small next to Europe and America, and import lots per product are limited. Small-lot importing cannot spread fixed costs such as clearance and approval thinly, so economies of scale barely operate. Ministry of Finance materials likewise report businesses for which approval of a revised list price could not keep pace with surging procurement prices, and which abandoned the application itself and stopped importing the product (Ministry of Finance Financial Bureau, 2024). Fewer products mean narrower choice — a vicious circle that readily sharpens the sense of dearness.\n【Comparisons with local prices】Even at tourist-facing prices at Havana's 'La Casa del Habano', a Montecristo No. 2 was reported at about $21.50 and a Cohiba Siglo VI in the $101 range as of 2024 — by no means cheap even at origin. Import prices for Cuban cigars themselves are said to have risen about 30% over the past decade, and in recent years by more than 20% a year for some marques (Ministry of Finance materials and trade press, 2020s; subject to change). With 16% import duty, weight-proportional tobacco tax and consumption tax layered on top, domestic list prices tend to settle a notch above local ones.\n【The pitfalls of parallel and personal imports】Personal importing through overseas online shops looks cheap at first glance, but delivery takes weeks, drying and storage conditions cannot be known, and there is no guarantee of authenticity. Cases of fakes entering the distribution chain, and of unexpected extra tax because the cigars weighed more than assumed, have also been noted. Counting in the lack of the warranty an official shop would provide, the official route has the advantage on condition and authenticity." },
  { h: "The cost anatomy of a cigar — leaf, labour, ageing, brand",
    body: "The price tag on a single cigar is the layered product of costs of quite different characters: procuring the wrapper leaf, hand-rolling labour, the time spent ageing, and the aura of the brand. Break them down in turn and a structure emerges in which most of the price traces back not to 'materials and manufacture' but to 'time and scarcity'.\n【The cost gap in wrapper leaf】The variable that moves the price most is the wrapper (the outer leaf), which decides most of a cigar's appearance and flavour. Connecticut Shade, grown under cloth tents in the Connecticut River Valley of the United States to block the sun, yields little and runs a high risk of disease; the top grades fetch around $50–55 per pound, and the industry rates it among the most expensive agricultural products in the world (Cigar Aficionado, 2004 and others). Shade-grown Honduran leaf, by contrast, runs to roughly $20–25 per pound, and sun-grown broadleaf is said to be cheaper still, so the wrapper alone opens a severalfold gap in cost. What is more, the usable yield per pound is poor, and the wrapper cost per cigar is said in some cases to reach several tens of cents.\n【Hand-rolling in practice, and rollers' wages】The heart of a premium cigar is the skill of the trained roller (torcedor) doing 'totalmente a mano' (fully handmade) work. Proficiency takes years, but the wages are a tiny fraction of the final price. One estimate from Cuba puts monthly pay, base plus piece-rate, at only around $57–77 (Entre Humos, c. 2023); Nicaraguan rollers are said to earn two to five times that, still far removed from the price the finished product commands. Of a stick selling for thousands or tens of thousands of yen, the share returned to the roller's labour is small; what pushes the price up is less the labour cost itself than the ageing and scarcity premiums below.\n【Ageing as a time cost】The leaf takes months to ferment and one to six years to age — longer still for some marques. Throughout, the maker pays for climate-controlled warehousing and insurance and shoulders the opportunity cost of capital that could otherwise have been realised by selling. The longer capital sits locked up as inventory, the more cost accrues and is passed into the final price — the payment for 'time resting the leaf' that accounts for part of the price gap between the mid and upper classes.\n【The brand and scarcity premium (Veblen goods)】The marques of state-owned Habanos S.A. in particular take on the character of luxury goods whose demand is propped up by high prices (so-called Veblen goods), with production restricted to preserve scarcity. Cohiba uses the medio tiempo leaf that grows only sparsely at the top of the plant, justifying its price with its storytelling as the flagship. When Habanos 'normalised' its prices to a Hong Kong benchmark in 2022, top marques such as Behike leapt past $100 a stick (Cigar Aficionado, 2025). Limited and discontinued marques become objects of speculation, and the price tag reflects brand prestige more than quality.\n【Why machine-made is cheap】By contrast, a machine-made dry cigar is assembled by automated machinery from chopped short filler with reconstituted-leaf (HTL) wrapper and binder, mass-produced at speeds above 1,000 sticks an hour. With little need for skilled labour or long ageing, it comes in at under $1 to a few dollars apiece. The wide price gap with hand-rolled premiums is thus a matter not only of leaf quality but of whether the surcharges of labour intensity, ageing and brand are present at all." },
  { h: "Spotting counterfeits and judging a fair price",
    body: "With cigars — Cuban Habanos above all — price is the first clue for spotting a fake. An extreme bargain should be read as a sign of risk, not of quality.\n【Why fakes arise】Cuban production is limited while worldwide demand runs high, and in the United States the embargo means no legitimate distribution exists at all, making Cubans a 'forbidden fruit' and a prime target for counterfeiting. Cigar Aficionado has cited estimates that 80–90% of 'Cuban' cigars circulating in the United States and Mexico are fake, and that in street sales in tourist spots such as Havana and Cancún the share approaches 100%. Especially targeted are the expensive and iconic Cohiba, and the Edición Limitada releases, said to be issued in only three marques a year.\n【The danger signals in a price】Cuban cigars are scarce luxury goods, and as a rule the genuine article is never far below the going rate. Half the going rate or less, or patter such as 'genuine stock diverted from the factory' or 'a relative works there and slips them out the back door', are classic tricks played on tourists and can be taken as near-certain signs of a fake. The thrill of having 'got it cheap' is precisely the trap that most dulls sober judgement.\n【Recognising official distribution and accredited shops】Habanos flow only through the official sales network. The official franchise 'La Casa del Habano', said to number more than 140 shops worldwide, and the 'Habanos Specialist' shops accredited by each country's official distributor, are the marks of trust; such shops are also noted for proper temperature and humidity control in walk-in humidors. In Japan the safe course is to buy through the official distributor and accredited online shops; US residents should note that personally importing Cuban cigars is itself illegal.\n【Boxes, seals and bands as guides】A genuine box bears the Warranty Seal, which from around 2009–2010 is said to have gained a hologram and a unique barcode. The seal is designed to self-destruct if peeled, and the hologram is said to have optical features such as two-colour 2D/3D imagery — a flat, merely printed texture is suspect. Other guides include the Denominación de Origen Protegida sticker, the spelling and typefaces of 'Hecho en Cuba' and 'Totalmente a mano' on the underside of the box, the embossing of the bands and their alignment inside the box. Transparent or glass-lidded boxes are held to be non-genuine. No individual feature is conclusive on its own, though — judge them in the round.\n【Verification, payment and websites】The barcode can reportedly be checked against Habanos' official barcode verification service (verificacion.habanos.com and the like), but the number itself indicates neither the box date nor the factory, and dealers have scope to reuse genuine numbers, so it is no panacea. On payment, the typical danger signals are said to include shunning credit cards in favour of bank transfer, cryptocurrency or gift cards only; imposing purchase minimums (five to ten boxes or more); missing contact details or the disclosures required under the Specified Commercial Transactions Law; and newly registered domains.\n【How to think about a fair price】Judging a fair price starts with 'knowing the going rate'. Anchor on the prices displayed by official distributors and accredited online shops, and treat any price far below them with suspicion. Final authentication is difficult even for experts; the surest course comes down to buying through a trusted official channel." },
  { h: "Value for money, the secondary market and investment",
    body: "Whether a cigar is 'dear' or 'cheap' cannot be measured by the list price alone; weigh whether it is worth the price once the time you get to enjoy it and the satisfaction it brings are counted in.\n【How to measure value】A simple yardstick is 'smoking time per stick'. A robusto is said to give roughly 45 minutes and a churchill some 60–90, so at the same price a larger vitola tends to cost less 'per hour'. But satisfaction cannot be measured in sticks or length: it is not unusual for a short but concentrated smoke to outrank a long, flat one. The realistic method is to set smoking time, price and felt satisfaction against one another by your own standards — and to count storage costs and travel time into the total cost as well.\n【How ageing changes value】Rested in the right conditions, a cigar loses its rough edges as the 'marrying' of the freshly rolled leaves progresses. Time raises both taste and value — but not every cigar improves with age, and the marques with real underlying power, the full-bodied ones, are said to have the most room to grow. Bear in mind that the value only emerges once the effort and years of ageing are added on top of the purchase price.\n【The secondary and vintage market】In the secondary market for scarce, ready-made stock, the central presence is C.Gars Ltd. of the United Kingdom, the world's only auction house devoted to cigars. It handles pre-embargo stock, Cuban-era Davidoff and Dunhill, and rare Cohibas; sale rates are reported to exceed 90% in some sessions, with one 2015 sale recording 99%. According to managing director Mitchell Orchant, the keenest bidding interest gathers around the pre-embargo lots.\n【The special case of charity auctions】The pinnacle is the annual 'Humidor Charity Auction' on the closing night of the Habanos Festival in Havana, whose proceeds are said to be donated to Cuban public health (the medical system). In 2024 a Cohiba humidor set a new single-lot record at about €4.5 million, with the night's total reported at about €17.8 million. In 2025 the Behike 15th-anniversary humidor raised the single-lot record again at about €4.6 million, but the night's total was put at about €16.25–16.41 million, down on the previous year. Single-lot records and the night's total are different things; figures vary between reports, and the totals do not necessarily keep rising. Understand, too, that hammer prices mix in topicality and brand prestige and do not reflect the merit of the contents alone.\n【What commands high prices】The common threads are small limited runs (Edición Limitada / Regional Editions), discontinued or ended production, the pre-embargo era, and clear provenance. Prices leap when the storytelling of an anniversary or the craft value of a bespoke humidor is added on.\n【Caution when viewing cigars as investments】Unlike wine, cigars lack systematic market data, and pricing is close to one price per single item. Liquidity is low, quick realisation difficult; high-priced Habanos are rife with forgery, and a mistake over authenticity wipes out the whole stake. Chasing appreciation demands the patience and care to lay stock down for ten years or more. Enjoyment first, asset value second — that is the healthy order in which to approach it. Note: all prices and hammer figures are approximate, and accounts vary." },
  /* TOPICS_END */
  ];
  if (D.priceTopics && D.priceTopics.length === TOPICS.length)
    D.priceTopics.forEach(function (t, i) { t.h = TOPICS[i].h; t.body = TOPICS[i].body; });
})();
